--- SECTION 1: HALBACH ARRAY MAGNETIC LEVITATION PHYSICS ---

HALBACH ARRAY CONFIGURATION:
  A Halbach array concentrates magnetic flux on one side:
  B(x) = B₀ · exp(-k·y) · sin(k·x)  [field below array]
  Where k = 2π/λ [λ = spatial period of magnet array]
  
  Field enhancement factor: κ = sin(π/M)/(π/M)
  M = number of magnet segments per period
  For M=4: κ ≈ 0.90 (90% of ideal)
  For M=8: κ ≈ 0.97 (97% of ideal)

LIFT FORCE CALCULATION:
  Pressure from Halbach: P_lift = B₀²/(2μ₀) · exp(-2k·g)
  Where g = levitation gap
  
  For neodymium N52 magnets:
  B_remanence = 1.44 T
  B₀ effective for Halbach: B₀ ≈ 1.2 T (for 4-segment array)
  
  Lift pressure at g=10mm: P = 1.2²/(2×4π×10⁻⁷) × exp(-2×(2π/0.05)×0.01)
                              = 573,248 Pa × exp(-2.51)
                              = 573,248 × 0.081
                              = 46,432 Pa
  
  Lift area needed for 100kg: A = (100×9.81)/46432 = 0.021 m²
  Practical area: ~0.2-0.5 m² (includes safety margins)
  Achievable lift: 100-250 kg at 10mm gap with reasonable magnet array

--- SECTION 2: PASSIVE VS ACTIVE LEVITATION ---

EARNSHAW'S THEOREM:
  "A static equilibrium of a charged particle in an electrostatic field
  is impossible" - extends to static magnetic levitation.
  
  Pure permanent magnet levitation: UNSTABLE (Earnshaw's theorem)
  
  EXCEPTIONS AND SOLUTIONS:
  1. Diamagnetic materials (bismuth, pyrolytic graphite)
     χ_m < 0: F = (χ_m/μ₀) · V · B · ∇B
     Required field gradient: very large (10 T² /m for graphite)
  
  2. Induced currents (Lenz's law) - requires motion
     Maglev trains use this: v_critical = R/(2L) ≈ 5 m/s
     Above v_critical: net lift force
  
  3. Active feedback control (our chosen approach)
     PID controller: u(t) = Kp·e(t) + Ki·∫e(t)dt + Kd·de/dt
     Gap sensor → controller → electromagnet current
     Stable equilibrium maintained by active correction

ACTIVE STABILIZATION DESIGN:
  Sensor: Hall effect (sensitivity ~50 mV/mT)
  Controller: Arduino/Raspberry Pi (1kHz update rate)
  Actuator: electromagnet coils (correction force ±10N)
  Power: 50-100W (correction only, permanent magnets carry load)
  
  Transfer function:
  G(s) = K·exp(-τs)/(s²) [double integrator + delay]
  PID gain selection: Kp=20, Ki=5, Kd=10 [tuned for 1 rad/s bandwidth]
  Stability margin: 45° phase margin (robust)

--- SECTION 3: LINEAR MOTOR PROPULSION ---

LINEAR SYNCHRONOUS MOTOR:
  Force: F = (3/2) · (π/τ) · B_gap² · A_pole · I_peak · cos(φ)
  Where:
    τ = pole pitch (m)
    B_gap = air gap flux density (T)
    A_pole = pole face area (m²)
    φ = power factor angle
  
  Efficiency: η = P_mechanical / P_electrical = F·v / (V·I·cos φ)
  Target: η ≥ 85-90%
  
  For hover bike propulsion:
  Required thrust at 50 km/h: F = m·a + F_drag
  F_drag = 0.5·ρ·Cd·A·v² = 0.5×1.225×0.3×0.8×(13.9)² = 28 N
  Acceleration force: m·a = 90×0.5 = 45 N
  Total thrust: ~73 N
  Power at cruise: P = F·v = 28×13.9 = 389 W ✓ (within budget)

